    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">The Investment Property Requirement That Blindsides First-Time Investors: Cash Reserves</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you've saved up your down payment for an investment property and you're feeling ready to move, this article is for you, because there's a second number most first-time investors don't find out about until they're already in the application process, and it derails more deals than bad credit does.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Number Nobody Warns You About</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Down payment gets all the attention. Reserves get almost none. But after you hand over your down payment, most lenders require you to have an additional several months of mortgage payments sitting in liquid accounts, per financed property. Not locked up in a retirement account or tied up in equity somewhere. Liquid. Accessible. Documented. If you've drained your savings to cover the down payment and closing costs, you may not qualify, even if your credit is strong and the property cash flows well.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why the System Rewards Wealth, Not Just Readiness</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's the problem no one names directly. The reserve requirement isn't really about your ability to make payments. It's about the lender's confidence that you can survive a disruption: an empty unit, a major repair, a slow month. Lenders want to see a buffer, and that logic is reasonable. The problem is it creates a structural disadvantage for first-generation investors who've done everything right but concentrated their savings into the down payment itself.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A borrower with a large brokerage account can acquire multiple investment properties in the same year without stress. A borrower who worked hard to save a down payment might not qualify despite being equally creditworthy, simply because after closing there isn't enough left in liquid accounts to satisfy the reserve requirement.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For many investors, the barrier isn't income or credit. It's the stack of liquid assets remaining after you close.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How Cash Reserve Requirements for Investment Property Loans Actually Break Down</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Reserve requirements vary by lender and loan program, but here are realistic benchmarks for conventional investment property loans.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For a single investment property, most lenders require several months of total monthly payment in reserves, documented after closing costs and down payment are accounted for. If you also carry a mortgage on your primary residence, lenders typically require additional reserves on that property as well.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">If you're financing multiple investment properties or already have existing financed rentals, reserve requirements stack. Some lenders calculate required reserves across your entire portfolio of financed properties. The more properties you carry, the more liquid capital you need to demonstrate before a new loan gets approved.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">DSCR lenders tend to be somewhat more flexible on reserves than conventional lenders, but a meaningful cushion is still standard. Hard money lenders care less about reserves but charge significantly more in rate and fees to compensate.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">What typically counts as reserves: checking and savings accounts, money market accounts, stocks and bonds, and vested retirement funds at a discounted percentage to account for early withdrawal penalties. What generally does not count: equity in real estate, unvested retirement funds, or funds that were gifted specifically for this transaction.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Calculate your full reserve requirement before you make an offer.</strong> Estimate several months of total payment on the target property, plus reserves on your primary residence and any existing financed investment properties.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not drain your savings to hit a precise down payment number.</strong> If a larger down payment leaves you short on reserves, run both scenarios. Sometimes a slightly smaller down payment with adequate reserves is the stronger application.</li>
      <li><strong>Start building reserves at least 6 to 12 months before you apply.</strong> Lenders will ask for several months of bank statements. A large sudden deposit right before application raises underwriting questions. Consistent, documented savings history is what underwriters want to see.</li>
      <li><strong>Document every asset account before you apply.</strong> Gather statements for all checking, savings, brokerage, and retirement accounts. Know your total liquid position before your lender asks for it.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask about reserve requirements explicitly when comparing lenders.</strong> This number is not standardized. One lender may require more months of reserves than another, and that difference can determine whether you qualify at all.</li>
    </ol>

